WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.520
SERGEI SOKOLENKO: Hello, and
welcome to Real-Time AI--

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.610
Bringing Together Dataflow,
TensorFlow Extended,

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.190
and Cloud AI.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.980
My name is Sergei Sokolenko,
and I'm a Product Manager

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.120
in the Google Cloud.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.120
I wanted to start with
a motivating example.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.760
Recently worked with a
public safety agency which

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.900
processes tons of gigabytes
of security logs per day,

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.299
but then only a few
thousand of these logs

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:34.400
are of high importance to them.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.890
The current processes
for analysis and finding

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:40.370
these important logs were
based on heuristics and manual

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.740
interactions, which
couldn't scale

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.690
to the growing volume of logs.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.540
So this agency
engaged with Google

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.420
to process large scale
data sets and wanted

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.410
to build a unsupervised solution
to identify logs, review

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:55.400
them, and label them.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.350
In another example, we worked
with a national telecom,

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.110
which carries the critical
mobile traffic for our nation.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.770
This telecom needed to address
security, vulnerability,

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:10.090
among other threats.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.860
And their current
techniques, which

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.920
were based on traditional
signature-based patterns,

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.180
were insufficient.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.150
They just couldn't be
updated fast enough.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:22.360
So this telecom wanted to
analyze sender and receiver

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.860
traffic and find anomalies in
the traffic and act on them.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.820
The common factors
to these examples--

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.430
what is common to
them is that there

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:36.180
is a large number of events that
is produced by a global user

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.840
base, which creates billions
and billions of interactions

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.000
when they use these systems.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.180
Another important common
characteristic, a factor,

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.120
is that our customers
want to react

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.540
to these events with
near-human intelligence

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.930
and apply a higher
intelligence than just counting

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:59.990
numbers or creating and
calculating statistics.

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:02.750
And lastly, our
users want to react

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.048
to events in near real-time.

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:12.400
This is best visualized
by the following graphic.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.070
Businesses realize the
highest value from events

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:18.400
when they act within
a tight time window

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.930
after the event creation.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.770
Another opportunity to
extract value from events

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:27.750
is by storing these streaming
events in data warehouses

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.190
and doing analytics on them.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.930
In between, there isn't
much value to extract,

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.750
because data is stale or is
difficult to access and not

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:41.850
in a data warehouse, which means
it's not readily available.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.790
Across our customers, we see
two types of common needs--

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.430
recommendations and anomaly
and pattern detection.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.160
Recommendations are about
analyzing customer market

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.450
signals to present compelling
offers and customized content.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:57.610
For example, in the
retail industry,

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.700
you could create up-sell
offers for your carts.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.450
In the media industry, you could
select the next video or song

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.700
to show to a customer.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:10.480
In the financial industry, you
could suggest a new insurance

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:11.290
offer.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.420
And in the telecom
industry, you could

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:18.590
create a hardware offer or a
service up-sell or cross-sell.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:20.153
For anomalies and
pattern detection,

00:03:20.153 --> 00:03:21.570
here is what
customers want to do,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:26.760
is examining streams of data
to identify data points which

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.810
are outside of the norm.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.100
Now in the financial
industry, for example, you

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.920
could detect insider trading.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:39.350
In retail, you could find
account theft or credit card

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:40.220
fraud.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.770
In the cybersecurity
industry, as we've

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.810
seen in our motivating
example, you

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:47.690
could do a denial-of-service
attack prevention.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:50.850
And in healthcare, you
could do patient monitoring.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.620
And so if we want to
take these use cases

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.710
and if we want to act on them
to drive behavior, and not

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:03.090
just act and discover things
after the fact, when it's

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:06.180
too late, we need to use
machine learning and streaming

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.620
analytics technologies.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.470
And this is what
real-time AI is about.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.680
It's the difference between
preventing fraud and just

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.020
discovering fraud.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.640
It's the difference
between driving more sales

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:21.120
in shopping carts versus seeing
carts being abandoned when

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.670
customers move away from them.

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:27.550
Machine learning, together
with streaming analytics

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:31.720
and a global event network,
allows us to do real-time AI.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:38.240
The Google Cloud offers
a comprehensive set

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:41.550
of tools for model
training and inference.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:43.410
Big data, for
example, allows you

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:46.260
to do a model training
and predictions right

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:49.350
inside of your data warehouse.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:53.820
Outside of BigQuery, GCP has the
Google Cloud Platform, Cloud AI

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:57.120
Platform, and
TensorFlow Extended.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.840
Cloud AI Platform offers
training, inference,

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.520
and many specialized
services for video, image,

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.710
text processing, and other
types of unstructured data

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:10.090
processing.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.100
TensorFlow Extended
is a platform

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.590
for production use
of machine learning,

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:16.090
and it's centered
around TensorFlow,

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:18.170
but is available not
just on the Google Cloud.

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:24.490
What is common to all these
machine learning options

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:30.170
is the solid integration of
data flow as the data processor.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:33.640
For example, for BigQuery,
you can use streaming Dataflow

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.120
to onboard data into BigQuery,
and then preprocess it

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:39.540
before it lands there.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.190
Once the data is
in BigQuery, you

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:43.270
can train your
models using BQML,

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:48.240
email which is a variant of
SQL, and export between models

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.310
and use them inside of Dataflow
for streaming predictions.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.530
For the Cloud AI
Platform, Dataflow

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:58.650
integrates with Kubeflow, which
is the orchestrator of machine

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:00.760
learning pipelines.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.580
Cloud AI platform also
uses Dataflow internally

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:08.630
to do model training
on CPUs or GPUs.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:10.780
For TensorFlow
Extended, Dataflow

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:14.350
enables all large scale
data processing, both

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:18.630
for feature processing as
well as for model improvement.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.900
It can also bring
streaming events

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:22.945
into streaming inference.

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:30.380
Remember our motivating example
with the public safety agency?

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:33.190
Well, we worked with this
agency and created a solution

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:38.100
that is based on a time-series
anomaly detection solution

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:42.490
and is powered by Dataflow
and TensorFlow Extended.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:46.680
This solution uses LSTM
encoder-decoder pattern,

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:48.490
and it reduced
the number of logs

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:52.210
that needed to be monitored
manually from billions per day

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:53.920
to just a few thousand
per day, which

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:59.290
is doable for their
analytical workforce.

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:03.005
This solution is now available
on the cloud.google.com

00:07:03.005 --> 00:07:05.905
Dataflow in the
real-time AI section.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:11.640
The solution consists
of three steps.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:14.920
In the preprocessing
step, we convert log data

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:19.980
to a time-series TFRecord
formatted data set,

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:23.840
and we store it
in Cloud Storage.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:25.690
Once data lands
in Cloud Storage,

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.250
we begin the model
training step.

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:33.582
Now for this, we use the Cloud
AI Platform Training API.

00:07:33.582 --> 00:07:36.560
And this is where the
encoder-decoder LSTM

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:42.270
model gets created and stored
in the Cloud Storage again.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:45.520
A parallel Dataflow
pipeline is running

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.180
and consumes this
LSTM model, and then

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:54.830
scores each incoming
data record,

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:58.170
creates both a reconstructed
and actual time-series,

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:02.620
compares the two, and flags
differences and anomalies.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:05.080
The output is, again,
stored in Cloud Storage.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:10.540
In our second
motivating example,

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:13.420
we also developed an
anomaly detection solution

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:17.140
which processes streaming
events, call records

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:19.540
for this telecom,
and the processing

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:23.140
is happening in Dataflow, and
the online inference is also

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:26.300
happening inside of Dataflow.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:30.730
We use BigQuery ML to train
a K-means Cluster Model,

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:34.490
and we also use
Cloud to de-identify

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:37.340
and anonymize network
traffic records

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:41.970
before they are being
used for training.

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.340
Once the BQML model
is trained, it

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:47.010
gets deployed into
Streaming Dataflow,

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:51.320
and it's used for detecting
anomalies in real-time.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:54.340
This solution is also
available on our website,

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:58.370
cloud.google.com/dataflow,
Real-time AI.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:04.320
Here's a graph
that represents how

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:08.480
an anomaly gets detected using
a K-means cluster approach.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:10.860
The solution finds
outliers by measuring

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:15.120
the distance between them and
the centers of the clusters.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.520
Outliers that have a
distance of more than three

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:20.280
standard deviations
of the cluster

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:22.240
are considered anomalous.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:29.440
This solution is also structured
into the three steps--

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:32.560
the feature preprocessing
step, the model training step,

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:35.220
and the anomaly reduction step.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.470
In the feature
preprocessing step,

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:39.600
Dataflow is reading
data from Pub/Sub,

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.530
the call records, and is
using Cloud DLP to anonymize

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:47.020
the incoming records.

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:50.380
All call detail records
in an anonymous form

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:53.220
are stored in
BigQuery, and we use

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:58.030
BigQuery ML to train a model
that creates a K-means cluster

00:09:58.030 --> 00:09:59.830
model.

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:03.780
This model is then used in a
parallel Dataflow pipeline,

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:07.680
which reads the same call
detail records and scores them

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:10.190
against the model.

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:14.400
The anomalies that are found
are outputted into Pub/Sub

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.410
for further action.

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:18.360
The solution is
available on our website

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:20.170
under the real-time section.

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:23.051
Feel free to discover
and customize.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:29.270
In addition to the two existing
solutions that I just covered,

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:33.920
today, we are announcing new
components and new solutions.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:37.350
We created, recently, new
Apache Beam components--

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:38.780
we call them transforms--

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:42.740
that integrate data flow
better with Cloud AI platform.

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:45.440
These components are
for video analysis,

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:48.680
for image analysis,
text analysis, DLP,

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:51.430
and many other areas.

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:54.450
We also created a
TFX component can

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:57.990
be embedded into Beam pipelines
for streaming predictions,

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:01.950
either on locally loaded
models or on remote models.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:04.820
And the API stays the same.

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:06.550
In addition to the
new components,

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:10.670
we are also releasing today
five new solutions for anomaly

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:12.830
and pattern detection.

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:16.190
You can use these solutions
as is, you can customize them,

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:18.860
or you can use them as
examples for the components

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:22.513
that we developed
and provided for you.

00:11:22.513 --> 00:11:23.930
Let's see what
these solutions do.

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:31.070
These solutions allow you to do
three types of things-- pattern

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:34.830
detection, anomaly detection,
and predictive forecasting.

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:36.740
For example, for
pattern detection,

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:40.440
you can find objects where
they're not supposed to be.

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:44.390
You can use our solutions
to anonymize and de-identify

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:49.470
operational data, a very
common need by users.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:52.610
You can also use our
solutions to reduce shrinkage,

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:55.080
by tracking packages
door to door.

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:58.370
Those are just a few
examples of the application

00:11:58.370 --> 00:12:00.880
of our solutions.

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:04.390
For anomaly detection
in the telecom case,

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:07.330
you can find malware by
detecting anomalies in traffic

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:10.300
between senders and recipients.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:14.340
For financial services, you can
find anomalies in currency pair

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:16.550
and stock trades.

00:12:16.550 --> 00:12:20.750
You can also find fraudulent
transactions or money transfers

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:23.610
in an account.

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:26.430
For predictive forecasting,
you can use our solutions

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:27.810
for equipment maintenance.

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:31.080
For example, you can forecast
the probability of equipment

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:33.420
failure based on
images, and then

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:37.770
send technicians to address
those forecasted failure

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:38.780
conditions.

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:44.810
Let's look at our
first solution.

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:48.350
The solution allows you to
detect patterns in video clips.

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:52.370
And here we integrated with the
Cloud Video Intelligence API

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:56.120
for object recognition.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:59.880
As data gets-- as video clips
get stored in Cloud Storage,

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:03.440
we get a notification about
these clips through Pub/Sub

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:07.520
into Dataflow, and the clips
get uploaded into Dataflow

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:11.840
and processed through the
Video Intelligence API.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:15.140
Once we know what objects are
inside of the video clips--

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:22.100
they can be cars, cats,
persons, or moving objects--

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:24.560
the labeling information
is stored together

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:30.630
with the clip metadata, either
into BigQuery or into Pub/Sub.

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:33.020
Dataflow here is used
for several things.

00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:36.640
For example, we do
smart clip partitioning,

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:39.550
so that a one-minute
long clip gets

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:42.170
processed in just a few seconds.

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:45.860
Dataflow can also do smart
batching of video clips

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:48.260
to fully exhaust
your available quota

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:51.060
and increase your throughput.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:55.350
You can use file inputs as
well as real-time inputs,

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:58.260
meaning Pub/Sub
messages, to indicate

00:13:58.260 --> 00:14:01.140
that a new clip is available.

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:06.570
And information about labels can
be stored in data warehouses,

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:10.170
in file systems, or in
messaging [? boxes. ?]

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:13.640
This solution, as the
previous two solutions,

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:15.990
is available on
our website and can

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:19.680
be viewed in the Real-time AI
section on the Dataflow page.

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:26.540
In our second new
solution, we allowed

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:30.980
you to predict equipment
failure by using and analyzing

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:34.070
the images of this equipment.

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:37.610
Here, we integrated with
the Cloud Vision API,

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:41.480
and we looked for
patterns in images.

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:45.270
As data and as images get
uploaded to Cloud Storage,

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:48.120
notifications are
being sent to Dataflow.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:51.030
Dataflow reacts to the
availability of new images

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:54.730
and calls the Cloud Vision API.

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:57.120
And once we have the
labels and classifications

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:01.020
of objects inside of images,
we store this information

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:03.030
in BigQuery.

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:05.560
You can use this
solution today as is,

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:08.220
or you can customize and
change the pipeline logic.

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:10.230
Because in addition
to the solutions,

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:13.620
we also give you the
standardized Apache Beam

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.690
transforms, which
you can integrate

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:19.080
in any kind of pipeline
you have to be.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:23.960
You can also save costs through
Dataflow's smart batching

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:24.800
approach.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:28.610
Dataflow is using its
State and Timer API

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:32.570
to convert a stream of image
files into micro batches

00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:35.450
and calls the API,
the Cloud Vision API,

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:39.277
efficiently and fully exhausts
the quota and the capacity

00:15:39.277 --> 00:15:39.860
that you have.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:46.340
In our next solution,
we allowed customers

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:49.910
to anonymize and
de-identify PII data.

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:53.120
And here, we integrated
with Cloud DLP,

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:59.450
which is Google Cloud's service
for anonymizing private PII

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:01.130
data.

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:03.550
The service also
provides tokenization,

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:05.720
de-identification,
and other services.

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:12.560
Dataflow here is used to
maximize the throughput

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:15.470
of the Cloud DLP API.

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:19.100
Again, we're using the Dataflow
state and timer capabilities

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:23.690
to smartly batch together
calls to the Cloud DLP API

00:16:23.690 --> 00:16:26.820
and use your
resources efficiently.

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:29.110
You can use file sources.

00:16:29.110 --> 00:16:31.190
You can use real time sources.

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:35.410
And clean data can be stored in
a variety of outputs, including

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:41.550
BigQuery, Pub/Sub, Cloud
Storage, and other things.

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:45.600
This solution, as many others,
is available on our website.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.400
Please navigate to
the Dataflow web page

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:49.680
and review the
real-time AI section.

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:56.780
In our next solution, we
implemented and integrated

00:16:56.780 --> 00:17:00.530
with Cloud AI Prediction
API to find anomalies

00:17:00.530 --> 00:17:03.360
in financial transactions.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:05.579
This integration is
not just specific

00:17:05.579 --> 00:17:08.010
to the financial
services industry.

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:12.220
You can use this pattern
across many, many industries.

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:14.829
Our particular model
to find anomalies

00:17:14.829 --> 00:17:19.440
is based on BoostedTrees, so
it gives you another pattern

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:24.849
to put into your toolbox of
anomaly detection solutions.

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:28.660
The model was trained in
the Cloud AI Training API

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:34.290
and deployed and hosted into
the Cloud AI Prediction API.

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:36.150
Dataflow, as in
many other cases,

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:39.650
is used to optimize
performance and throughput.

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:42.360
It micro-batches
calls to Cloud AI,

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:44.790
and it increases the
throughput of your system.

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:52.400
In our last solution, we're
using yet another approach

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:54.170
for anomaly detection.

00:17:54.170 --> 00:17:56.900
Here, we integrated
with TensorFlow Extended

00:17:56.900 --> 00:18:00.110
and use the Run Inference
transform that was recently

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:02.050
developed and released.

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:05.660
This transform allows you
to have your models hosted

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:09.335
both inside of Dataflow, as
well as remotely in Cloud AI

00:18:09.335 --> 00:18:12.020
Prediction.

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:16.580
Before we use Prediction
API, data actually

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:17.900
needs to be processed.

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:23.060
And here's where Dataflow's
strengths are coming to play.

00:18:23.060 --> 00:18:27.770
Data gets onboarded-- raw data
from external data providers

00:18:27.770 --> 00:18:30.440
gets onboarded through Pub/Sub.

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:32.580
And it gets aggregated
in Dataflow.

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:37.460
Specifically, we divide raw
trades into time windows

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:40.340
and run a variety of statistics
on them, for example,

00:18:40.340 --> 00:18:42.860
calculate means,
candle statistics,

00:18:42.860 --> 00:18:47.330
as well as the RSI stats, which
stands for Relative Strength

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:53.780
Index and is an indicator
of movement of data.

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:58.130
The preprocessed
data records get

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:02.210
transformed into TFX
[? sample ?] records

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:04.760
and stored in Cloud
Storage, from where

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:07.850
we pick up yet
another TFX pipeline

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:10.760
and use Cloud AI
Training to create

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:13.940
the model for the actual
anomaly detection.

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:18.710
This model is also based on
LSTM encoder-decoder approach.

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:21.230
It's trained in
Cloud AI Training,

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:23.520
and you can deploy
it in two places.

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:26.540
You can deploy it either
in Dataflow itself,

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:29.300
on the Dataflow workers
for local inference, which

00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:33.230
reduces latency, or you
can deploy this model

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:37.670
in Cloud AI Prediction
API for remote inference.

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:41.120
The interface through these
calls is always the same.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:44.290
This is where you can use
our newly developed TFX Run

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:47.230
Inference transform.

00:19:47.230 --> 00:19:49.270
This solution, as
many others, is

00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:51.790
available on the
Dataflow web page

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:53.560
under the real-time AI section.

00:19:57.150 --> 00:20:01.020
Just to illustrate what Dataflow
does to preprocessed raw stock

00:20:01.020 --> 00:20:05.130
trades before they get
used in the training

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:09.840
on the anomaly detection model,
take a look at this graph.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:15.360
As raw events representing
trades enter the pipeline,

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:19.350
we divide them into time
windows, represented

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:21.990
by accumulators on
this graph, and we

00:20:21.990 --> 00:20:25.360
calculate rolling windows
statistics on them.

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:29.370
For example, we calculate the
mean of these time windows,

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:34.290
and we calculate dependent
and other statistics,

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:36.720
for example, the RSI index.

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:39.810
Again, RSI stands for
Relative Strength Index.

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:46.200
With this, I would like
to wrap up my session

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:51.450
and just go through the
main points of the session.

00:20:51.450 --> 00:20:53.820
As you've seen, you
can use Dataflow today

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:56.880
for data processing with a
variety of machine learning

00:20:56.880 --> 00:21:01.140
technologies, including BigQuery
ML, Cloud AI, and TensorFlow

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:02.770
Extended.

00:21:02.770 --> 00:21:05.450
We've recently
launched new Apache

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:10.320
Beam transforms for video,
images, DLP, and text

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:12.360
processing.

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:16.170
We also released a new TFX
transform that integrates

00:21:16.170 --> 00:21:20.330
with Beam for generalized
streaming predictions.

00:21:20.330 --> 00:21:23.460
And we launched and then
released five new solutions

00:21:23.460 --> 00:21:28.860
for anomaly detection
and pattern recognition.

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:30.930
If you would like to
learn more, please go

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:38.170
to cloud.google.com/dataflow and
find the real-time AI section.

00:21:38.170 --> 00:21:39.760
Thank you for
watching this session.

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:41.590
I hope you found it informative.

00:21:41.590 --> 00:21:43.550
Till next time.

